# OnionOverVPN — Tor configuration for the server container. TEMPLATE:
# @WG_SERVER_IP@ is substituted by entrypoint.sh from the environment, so this
# project holds no tunnel address in its code. Set it in .env, not here.
#
# This is the onion-routing daemon only. No browser, no GUI: it builds Tor
# circuits and exposes them as a transparent proxy, a DNS resolver and a SOCKS5
# port, all bound to the WireGuard address @WG_SERVER_IP@.
#
# Nothing listens on the container's bridge interface, so this box can never be
# used as an open proxy from the internet.

# Transparent proxy for the TCP that nftables redirects here.
# IsolateClientAddr gives each VPN client its own circuits; IsolateClientProtocol
# keeps DNS streams off the circuits used for TCP. For stronger unlinkability add
# IsolateDestAddr IsolateDestPort, at a real cost in speed (a new circuit per
# destination).
TransPort @WG_SERVER_IP@:9040 IsolateClientAddr IsolateClientProtocol

# DNS resolved inside Tor. Client configs point DNS= at @WG_SERVER_IP@:53, which the
# nftables rules redirect here. This is what keeps lookups off the clear net.
DNSPort @WG_SERVER_IP@:5353

# Direct SOCKS5, for apps that can proxy themselves rather than be redirected
# transparently. Always preferable when the app supports it: the app hands Tor a
# hostname instead of resolving it first. The client container re-exposes this
# port on the host as a ready-to-use SOCKS5 proxy.
SocksPort @WG_SERVER_IP@:9050 IsolateClientAddr IsolateClientProtocol

# Make .onion addresses work over the tunnel: a lookup returns a virtual IP from
# this range and Tor maps it back to the hidden service on connect. This range is
# Tor's own documented default, not a setting of ours — but it must not overlap
# the tunnel subnet, so check it if you move the tunnel into 10.192.0.0/10.
VirtualAddrNetworkIPv4 10.192.0.0/10
AutomapHostsOnResolve 1
AutomapHostsSuffixes .onion,.exit

# The tunnel is IPv4-only (client IPv6 is captured by the tunnel and dropped),
# so don't return AAAA answers no client can reach.
ClientUseIPv4 1
ClientUseIPv6 0

# Control port for `onionvpn newnym`, which asks Tor for fresh circuits so new
# connections leave from a different exit relay. Bound to the container's
# loopback, so it is reachable only via `docker exec` — never from the tunnel
# and never from the network. Cookie auth means there is no password to manage.
ControlPort 127.0.0.1:9051
CookieAuthentication 1

# Persisted on a named volume, so a restart reuses the cached consensus and
# bootstraps in seconds instead of minutes.
DataDirectory /var/lib/tor

# stdout goes to `docker logs`. The file is what the healthcheck greps for
# "Bootstrapped 100%", and it lives on tmpfs on purpose: a persisted log would
# still show a stale success from the previous run.
Log notice stdout
Log notice file /run/tor/notices.log
SafeLogging 1
